multiagent/m1/l3/s2.body:
In any system with more than one agent, partial failure is the normal case, not the exception. A worker crashes mid-task, a tool call times out, a network hiccup drops a message. A robust orchestrator treats every dispatch as something that might not come back, and designs for recovery from the start.

Three techniques form the core toolkit. Timeouts bound how long the orchestrator waits for a worker before giving up, so one stuck agent cannot freeze the whole task. Retries re-dispatch a failed sub-task, often with exponential backoff — waiting 1s, then 2s, then 4s — so a briefly overloaded worker gets breathing room instead of a retry storm. A circuit breaker goes further: after repeated failures from one worker it stops sending traffic there entirely for a cooling-off period, protecting the system from a persistently sick component.

Retries only work if the operation is idempotent — safe to run more than once with the same effect as running it once. Reading a web page is naturally idempotent; sending an email or charging a card is not. To retry non-idempotent actions safely, attach an idempotency key (often the message_id) so the receiver can detect and ignore a duplicate rather than acting on it twice.

[python code]
def dispatch_with_retry(worker, task, max_attempts=3, timeout=30):
    for attempt in range(max_attempts):
        try:
            # idempotency_key lets the worker dedupe retries
            return worker.run(task, timeout=timeout,
                              idempotency_key=task.id)
        except (TimeoutError, WorkerError):
            if attempt == max_attempts - 1:
                raise
            backoff = 2 ** attempt  # 1s, 2s, 4s
            sleep(backoff)

